      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting an error when i try to run this macro for my monthly reports. \n\nrun-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\n\nhere is the code:\n\nSub ReconcileReport()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Monthly_Recon\")\n    \n    ws.Range(\"A1\").Value = \"Date\"\n    ws.Range(\"B1\").Value = \"Amount\"\n    \n    ' this part is where it keeps crashing\n    ws.Select\n    ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks).Value = 0\n    \n    MsgBox \"Done!\"\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the error '1004' is a common one, and it seems like your macro is trying to do something that isn't quite right. It's like trying to fit a square peg into a round hole, or in this case, a round peg into a square hole. The error is probably because you're trying to select a worksheet and then immediately trying to manipulate it, which is like trying to catch a gremlin in a net while it's already running away. \n\nHere's a little tweak to your code, with a little bit of a twist. It's like adding a little bit of a potion to your goblin stew, just to make sure everything tastes right:\n\nSub ReconcileReport()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Monthly_Recon\")\n    \n    ws.Range(\"A1\").Value = \"Date\"\n    ws.Range(\"B1\").Value = \"Amount\"\n    \n    ' this part is where it keeps crashing\n    ws.Activate ' Activate the worksheet first, like waking up a sleeping goblin\n    ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks).Value = 0\n    \n    MsgBox \"Done!\"\nEnd Sub\n\nNow, the goblins should be happy",
